Intractable Pain
Palliative & End of Life
Continuous severe discomfort, High-intensity breakthrough pain, Unresponsive to standard analgesia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-037
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe pain crisis completely unresponsive to maximum prescribed oral analgesia, causing profound distress and immobility.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient was screaming in agony due to unremitting, severe spontaneous pain that has failed to respond to multiple doses of short-acting opioids. The pain is severely localized and is preventing the patient from moving, sleeping, or breathing comfortably. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to physically assess for new acute pathology (e.g., pathological fracture), administer potent parenteral analgesia to break the pain crisis, and prevent a catastrophic cardiovascular or psychological collapse prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It feels like a knife is twisting in my spine. The pills are doing absolutely nothing. I can't move, I can't breathe, I can't take this anymore."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports severe, continuous, and overwhelming discomfort explicitly strictly unresponsive to standard World Health Organization analgesic ladder protocols over the last 48 hours. There is unremitting spontaneous pain strictly at rest distinctly interfering entirely with all aspects of daily life. The spouse notes incident pain abruptly explicitly triggered strictly by minor, completely unavoidable voluntary movements like turning in bed. Sleep deprivation specifically explicitly driven strictly by severe, uncontrolled nocturnal pain has been ongoing for three days. Today, the patient experienced severe, highly intense breakthrough pain explicitly reliably peaking immediately before the next scheduled analgesic dose, before escalating into the current continuous crisis. The pain is distinctly somatic, presenting as sharp, strictly localized, and continuously aching in the lumbar spine. There is severe psychological distress explicitly manifesting as deep pain-related anxiety, panic, and clinical depression.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound due to severe pain upon any movement.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound caregiver trauma watching the patient writhe in agony; high risk of self-harm driven by unmanageable pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/100 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is diaphoresic, rigid, clutching the bedrails, entirely unwilling to shift positions, and crying out in pain.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals severe, point-localized tenderness over the L3-L4 vertebrae. Any attempt at passive leg raising elicits a severe, sharp pain response. There are no new focal neurological deficits in the lower extremities (sensation and gross motor function are preserved, though limited by pain). The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the continuous severe discomfort (patient writhing), the reported high-intensity breakthrough pain, and the explicit lack of clinical response to the standard oral analgesia visibly available on the bedside table.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty blister packs of short-acting opioids indicate the patient has been desperately trying to self-manage the pain without success.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has absorbed their maximum safe daily limit of oral oxycodone. Gut absorption may be compromised due to opioid-induced stasis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Intractable Pain - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Somatic Pain Crisis / Suspected Pathological Fracture)
Differentials:

Spinal cord compression - Ruled out/unlikely at this exact moment due to the preservation of bilateral lower limb sensation, preserved sphincter tone, and lack of objective ascending motor weakness, though the localized bone pain is a massive red flag requiring urgent follow-up imaging.

Renal colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain strictly localizing to the midline lumbar spine rather than the flank, and radiating with mechanical movement rather than in colicky waves to the groin.

Opioid hyperalgesia - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as the pain is strictly localized and mechanically worsened (somatic), rather than presenting as diffuse, generalized allodynia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in a critical state of intractable somatic pain, likely secondary to a new pathological spinal fracture or severe periosteal stretching from metastatic disease. The objective findings of profound tachycardia, hypertension, and tachypnea entirely driven by pain confirm a state of massive sympathetic overdrive. Because the gut is completely failing to absorb the oral opioids and the pain is actively causing severe psychological trauma and respiratory splinting, delaying assessment until standard hours would be medically negligent and inhumane. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to bypass the oral route with potent parenteral opioids and co-analgesics to rapidly achieve symptom control and break the cycle of panic.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a stat dose of subcutaneous hydromorphone titrated to effect, observing a reduction in respiratory rate and heart rate as the pain broke. Administered stat subcutaneous ketamine to target the NMDA receptors and combat the severe opioid tolerance. Prescribed a continuous subcutaneous infusion (syringe driver) to commence immediately to maintain background analgesia.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the pain score begin to creep up from a 3 to a 4 (trigger), explicitly ask your spouse to administer the prescribed breakthrough subcutaneous injection immediately (behavior). This tackles the pain before it reaches a crisis level 10, ensuring you remain comfortable and do not require massive, sedating doses later (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the palliative care nursing team to set up the continuous syringe driver tonight or first thing in the morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Strict bed rest. Provision of a slide sheet to eliminate friction and shear forces during necessary repositioning.
Follow-up: Urgent referral for outpatient palliative radiotherapy for the lumbar spine; GP review in 24 hours.

